<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Surena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 52 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Surena was a Parthian general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-suren">House of Suren</a> in the service of King Orodes II. In 53 BCE at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a>, using <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> kept supplied with arrows by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> train and heavy cataphract cavalry, he destroyed a much larger Roman army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a>, who was killed. Soon afterwards Orodes, apparently fearing his power, had him put to death.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Surena, or Suren, was a Parthian spahbed, a general or commander, and the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-suren">House of Suren</a>. The name is the Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> form of Sūrēn, which comes from the Avestan sūra, meaning strong or exalted, and Suren remains a common name in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, writing about 125 years after the events in his Life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a>, called him an extremely distinguished man who ranked next after the king in wealth, birth and honour, and the foremost Parthian of his time in courage and ability. He wrote that Surena's army included many slaves, though the meaning of the words for slaves there is disputed, and he described his painted face and his hair parted in the fashion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, which belied his manly skill.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 54 BCE Surena commanded troops of Orodes II at the battle for the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucia">Seleucia</a>, a contest over the Arsacid succession after Mithridates IV had deposed Orodes, and he was instrumental in restoring Orodes to the throne. He was one of the most powerful men in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>, and he travelled with what one scholar, Gazerani, calls an unmistakably courtly retinue.</p><p>In 53 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> advanced on the western Parthian vassal territories, and Orodes II sent his cavalry under Surena to meet them. The armies met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Carrhae</a>, at Harrân in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. The superior equipment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a> and their tactic of luring the Romans into the middle of the desert let them defeat the numerically larger force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Marcus Licinius Crassus</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> speaks of 20,000 Roman dead and 10,000 prisoners. The victory echoed among the peoples of the East but brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsacid-dynasty">Arsacid dynasty</a> no territory and no decisive shift in the balance of power. Surena was then executed by Orodes II, most likely because the king saw his power and influence as a threat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Battle of Carrhae</a> is commonly seen as one of the earliest and most important battles between the Roman and Parthian empires and one of the most crushing defeats in Roman history. Surena died in 53 or 52 BCE. His place in the historical tradition has been described as curiously parallel to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a>. The last work of the 17th-century French dramatist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-corneille">Pierre Corneille</a>, a tragedy titled Suréna, is roughly based on his story.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Surena” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Carrhae in 53 BCE, the Parthian general <b>Surena</b> kept his horse archers supplied from a camel train of arrows, destroying a Roman army under Crassus.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. Died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
